{"id":"gen-1790967609-QWBi3isxFs935pap9MsB","object":"chat.completion","created":1790967609,"model":"cohere/north-mini-code:free","provider":"Cohere","system_fingerprint":null,"service_tier":null,"choices":[{"index":0,"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop","native_finish_reason":"complete","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5122\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            1,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5122\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5122\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}","refusal":null,"reasoning":"We need to solve a CSP with variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c10 as given. The goal: Enumerate all satisfying assignments for component right, i.e., variables e,f,g,h, sorted lexicographically. We must output a JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing the list of tuples (each tuple is a list of 4 integers) for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for (e,f,g,h) that can be extended to a full assignment for all variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe must output all satisfying tuples for component right (i.e., all possible (e,f,g,h) that can be part of at least one full solution). The problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\" So we need to compute the projection onto (e,f,g,h) of the solution set.\n\nWe need to find all solutions to the CSP (full assignments) and then collect distinct (e,f,g,h) tuples.\n\nWe need to produce exactly one JSON envelope matching the template, with the \"tuples\" list filled appropriately.\n\nWe must not include any prose, only JSON, no markdown fences.\n\nWe need to be careful to compute the solution set correctly.\n\nThe CSP has 10 constraints:\n\nc1: variables a,b; allowed pairs: (2,3), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (1,0). So allowed (a,b) pairs: (2,3), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (1,0). So b can be 3,1,1,2,0 respectively. So b can be {0,1,2,3} but only specific combos.\n\nc2: variables b,c; allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,1). So allowed (b,c): (3,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,1). So c can be 0,0,0,1,1 respectively. So c ∈ {0,1} (maybe also others? no, c only 0 or 1 in allowed pairs). So c must be 0 or 1.\n\nc3: variables c,d; allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,1), (0,1), (2,2), (1,0). So allowed (c,d): (0,2), (2,1), (0,1), (2,2), (1,0). So d can be 2,1,1,2,0 respectively. So d ∈ {0,1,2} but not 3? Actually d can be 0,1,2.\n\nc4: variables d,a; allowed pairs: (2,2), (3,1), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1). So allowed (d,a): (2,2), (3,1), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1). So a can be 2,1,3,0,1 respectively. So a ∈ {0,1,2,3} but only in combos.\n\nc5: variables e,f; allowed pairs: (1,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (3,0). So (e,f): (1,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (3,0). So f can be 1,2,3,2,0. So f ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nc6: variables f,g; allowed pairs: (1,0), (3,3), (0,3), (2,3), (0,1). So (f,g): (1,0), (3,3), (0,3), (2,3), (0,1). So g ∈ {0,3,3,3,1} => g ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nc7: variables g,h; allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,0), (3,2), (3,3), (0,3). So (g,h): (0,0), (1,0), (3,2), (3,3), (0,3). So h ∈ {0,0,2,3,3} => h ∈ {0,2,3}. Also h can be 0,2,3.\n\nc8: variables h,e; allowed pairs: (0,1), (1,0), (2,0), (0,0), (1,3). So (h,e): (0,1), (1,0), (2,0), (0,0), (1,3). So e ∈ {1,0,0,0,1} => e ∈ {0,1}. So e ∈ {0,1}.\n\nc9: variables b,e; allowed pairs: (3,1), (1,0). So (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,0). So b and e are tightly coupled.\n\nc10: variables d,g; allowed pairs: (2,0), (2,3). So (d,g) must be (2,0) or (2,3). So d must be 2, and g is 0 or 3.\n\nThus, c10 forces d=2. That is a strong constraint. Let's check c10: allowed pairs: [ [2,0], [2,3] ]. So d=2, g ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus d is fixed to 2. Good.\n\nNow c3: variables c,d: allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,1), (0,1), (2,2), (1,0). Since d=2, we need pairs where second component =2. Let's list allowed pairs with d=2:\n\n- (0,2) => c=0, d=2 works.\n- (2,1) => d=1, no.\n- (0,1) => d=1, no.\n- (2,2) => c=2, d=2 works.\n- (1,0) => d=0, no.\n\nSo allowed (c,d) with d=2 are (0,2) and (2,2). So c can be 0 or 2.\n\nThus c ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow c2: variables b,c: allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,1). So allowed (b,c) combos:\n\n- (3,0) => c=0, b=3\n- (0,0) => c=0, b=0\n- (2,0) => c=0, b=2\n- (1,1) => c=1, b=1\n- (2,1) => c=1, b=2\n\nBut we have c ∈ {0,2}. So c cannot be 1. So only pairs with c=0 are possible. So c must be 0. Because c=2 is not allowed in any pair in c2. Indeed c2 allowed c values are only 0 or 1. So c must be 0. So c=0.\n\nThus c=0.\n\nGiven c=0, from c2 we have allowed (b,c) pairs where c=0: (3,0), (0,0), (2,0). So b can be 3,0,2.\n\nThus b ∈ {0,2,3}.\n\nNow c1: variables a,b; allowed pairs: (2,3), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (1,0). So given b ∈ {0,2,3}, we need to find allowed a such that (a,b) is in allowed pairs.\n\nList allowed (a,b) pairs:\n\n- (2,3): a=2, b=3\n- (1,1): a=1, b=1 (b=1 not in our set)\n- (0,1): a=0, b=1 (b=1 not in set)\n- (3,2): a=3, b=2\n- (1,0): a=1, b=0\n\nThus possible combos with b ∈ {0,2,3}:\n\n- b=3 => a=2 (from (2,3))\n- b=2 => a=3 (from (3,2))\n- b=0 => a=1 (from (1,0))\n\nThus a is determined by b:\n\nIf b=3, a=2.\nIf b=2, a=3.\nIf b=0, a=1.\n\nThus a ∈ {1,2,3} correspondingly.\n\nNow c4: variables d,a: allowed pairs: (2,2), (3,1), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1). d=2. So allowed (d,a) pairs with d=2:\n\n- (2,2): a=2\n- (3,1): d=3, not relevant.\n- (0,3): d=0, not relevant.\n- (3,0): d=3, not relevant.\n- (2,1): a=1\n\nThus allowed combos with d=2: (2,2) => a=2, and (2,1) => a=1.\n\nThus a can be 1 or 2.\n\nBut from earlier, a is determined by b: if b=0 => a=1; if b=2 => a=3; if b=3 => a=2.\n\nNow we need a to be consistent with c4: a must be 1 or 2.\n\nThus the case b=2 (a=3) is invalid because a=3 not allowed by c4. So b cannot be 2. So b ∈ {0,3}.\n\nCorresponding a:\n\n- b=0 => a=1 (allowed)\n- b=3 => a=2 (allowed)\n\nThus b=0, a=1; or b=3, a=2.\n\nThus far we have:\n\nc=0, d=2, g ∈ {0,3} (from c10), c10: (d,g) must be (2,0) or (2,3). So g is either 0 or 3.\n\nNow c7: variables g,h; allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,0), (3,2), (3,3), (0,3). So given g ∈ {0,3}, we need allowed (g,h) pairs.\n\nIf g=0: allowed (0,0) and (0,3). So h can be 0 or 3.\n\nIf g=3: allowed (3,2) and (3,3). So h can be 2 or 3.\n\nThus h ∈ {0,3} if g=0; h ∈ {2,3} if g=3.\n\nNow c8: variables h,e; allowed pairs: (0,1), (1,0), (2,0), (0,0), (1,3). So allowed (h,e) combos:\n\n- (0,1): h=0, e=1\n- (1,0): h=1, e=0 (h=1 not in our possible h values)\n- (2,0): h=2, e=0\n- (0,0): h=0, e=0\n- (1,3): h=1, e=3 (h=1 not allowed)\n\nThus h can be 0,1,2. But from c7, h ∈ {0,3} or {2,3}. So possible combos:\n\n- If h=0: allowed e can be 1 (from (0,1)) or 0 (from (0,0)). So e ∈ {0,1} for h=0.\n\n- If h=2: allowed e must be 0 (from (2,0)). So e=0.\n\n- If h=3: not in c8 allowed pairs? Let's check c8: allowed pairs list does not include h=3. So h cannot be 3 because there is no allowed (3, e) pair. So h cannot be 3. So h must be 0 or 2.\n\nThus h ∈ {0,2}.\n\nThus from c7, we need to see which g values allow h=0 or h=2.\n\n- g=0: allowed h ∈ {0,3}. So h can be 0 (allowed) but not 2. So if g=0, h must be 0.\n\n- g=3: allowed h ∈ {2,3}. So h can be 2 (allowed) but not 0. So if g=3, h must be 2.\n\nThus we have two cases:\n\nCase 1: g=0, h=0.\nCase 2: g=3, h=2.\n\nNow c8 also gives e values for each h:\n\n- If h=0: e ∈ {0,1} (from (0,0) and (0,1)). So e can be 0 or 1.\n\n- If h=2: e=0 (from (2,0)). So e must be 0.\n\nThus:\n\nCase 1: (g=0, h=0) => e ∈ {0,1}.\nCase 2: (g=3, h=2) => e=0.\n\nNow c5: variables e,f; allowed pairs: (1,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (3,0). So (e,f) must be one of those.\n\nThus for each possible e, we need to find f.\n\nCase 1: e ∈ {0,1}.\n\n- If e=0: allowed (e,f) pairs: (0,2), (0,3). So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- If e=1: allowed (e,f) pairs: (1,1), (1,2). So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus for case 1 (g=0, h=0), possible (e,f) combos:\n\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,1)\n- (1,2)\n\nCase 2: e=0 (only). So (e,f) must be (0,2) or (0,3). So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus overall possible (e,f) combos across both cases:\n\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,1)\n- (1,2)\n\nNow c6: variables f,g; allowed pairs: (1,0), (3,3), (0,3), (2,3), (0,1). So (f,g) must be one of these.\n\nWe have g fixed to either 0 or 3.\n\nLet's list allowed (f,g) pairs:\n\n- (1,0): f=1, g=0\n- (3,3): f=3, g=3\n- (0,3): f=0, g=3\n- (2,3): f=2, g=3\n- (0,1): f=0, g=1 (g=1 not possible)\n\nThus for g=0, allowed f must be 1 (since only (1,0) matches). So if g=0, f must be 1.\n\nFor g=3, allowed f can be 3,0,2 (from (3,3), (0,3), (2,3)). So f ∈ {0,2,3} for g=3.\n\nNow combine with earlier possible (e,f) combos.\n\nCase 1: g=0, h=0, e ∈ {0,1}, f must be 1 (from c6). So f=1.\n\nNow check if (e,f) combos include f=1. Yes, (1,1) is allowed. Also (0,2) etc are not. So only e=1, f=1 works.\n\nThus in case 1, the only valid assignment is (e=1, f=1). Check c5: (1,1) is allowed. So case 1 yields (e=1,f=1,g=0,h=0). So tuple (e,f,g,h) = (1,1,0,0).\n\nCase 2: g=3, h=2, e=0, f ∈ {0,2,3} (from c6). But also need (e,f) pair to be allowed by c5. Since e=0, allowed (e,f) combos are (0,2) and (0,3). So f must be 2 or 3. So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus possible (e,f,g,h) combos in case 2:\n\n- (e=0, f=2, g=3, h=2) => (0,2,3,2)\n- (e=0, f=3, g=3, h=2) => (0,3,3,2)\n\nNow check c6 for each:\n\n- (f=2,g=3): allowed (2,3) is in c6. Yes.\n- (f=3,g=3): allowed (3,3) is in c6. Yes.\n\nThus both are valid.\n\nNow we also need to check c9: variables b,e; allowed pairs: (3,1) or (1,0). So (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,0). So we need to ensure that b and e satisfy this.\n\nWe have two possible b values: b=0 (with a=1) or b=3 (with a=2). And e values: in case 1 e=1; case 2 e=0.\n\nThus check:\n\n- Case 1: e=1, b can be? Options: b=0 or b=3. (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,0). So (b=3, e=1) matches (3,1). So b must be 3. b=0 would give (0,1) not allowed. So b must be 3. So case 1 forces b=3, a=2.\n\n- Case 2: e=0, b must be? (b,e) must be (1,0) (since (3,1) not possible). So b must be 1. But b can only be 0 or 3 from earlier constraints. So b=1 is not allowed. Therefore case 2 is invalid because b cannot be 1.\n\nThus only case 1 yields a valid full solution.\n\nThus the only full solution is:\n\n- b=3, a=2 (since b=3 => a=2 from c1)\n- c=0, d=2, g=0, h=0, e=1, f=1.\n\nCheck all constraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c1.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c2.\n\nc3: (c,d) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c3.\n\nc4: (d,a) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in c4.\n\nc5: (e,f) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c5.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in c6.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in c7.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is in c8.\n\nc9: (b,e) = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c9.\n\nc10: (d,g) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c10.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the only full solution yields right component tuple (e,f,g,h) = (1,1,0,0). So the projection onto right component has exactly one tuple: [1,1,0,0].\n\nThus the answer: payload.claim.value.tuples should contain a list with that tuple. Sorted lexicographically (only one). So we need to output JSON envelope with that tuple.\n\nThe envelope template includes fields: \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\", \"payload\": {...}, \"problem_id\": \"csp-5122\", \"read_refs\": [...], \"recipients\": [\"left-reviewer\",\"shared-integrator\"], \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"right-proposer\". The payload.claim.value.tuples currently has placeholder [[0,0,0,0]]; we need to replace with our tuple.\n\nWe must edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. So we produce the envelope with the tuple replaced.\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5122\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            1,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5122\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5122\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe must ensure proper JSON formatting: no trailing commas, proper quoting.\n\nThus final answer is exactly that JSON.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need to solve a CSP with variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. There are constraints c1..c10 as given. The goal: Enumerate all satisfying assignments for component right, i.e., variables e,f,g,h, sorted lexicographically. We must output a JSON envelope with payload.claim.value.tuples containing the list of tuples (each tuple is a list of 4 integers) for e,f,g,h.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for (e,f,g,h) that can be extended to a full assignment for all variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h satisfying all constraints.\n\nWe must output all satisfying tuples for component right (i.e., all possible (e,f,g,h) that can be part of at least one full solution). The problem says \"Enumerate ALL and ONLY satisfying assignments for component right, sorted lexicographically. Edit only payload.claim.value.tuples in the envelope. Each row contains exactly four domain integers in the listed variable order.\" So we need to compute the projection onto (e,f,g,h) of the solution set.\n\nWe need to find all solutions to the CSP (full assignments) and then collect distinct (e,f,g,h) tuples.\n\nWe need to produce exactly one JSON envelope matching the template, with the \"tuples\" list filled appropriately.\n\nWe must not include any prose, only JSON, no markdown fences.\n\nWe need to be careful to compute the solution set correctly.\n\nThe CSP has 10 constraints:\n\nc1: variables a,b; allowed pairs: (2,3), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (1,0). So allowed (a,b) pairs: (2,3), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (1,0). So b can be 3,1,1,2,0 respectively. So b can be {0,1,2,3} but only specific combos.\n\nc2: variables b,c; allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,1). So allowed (b,c): (3,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,1). So c can be 0,0,0,1,1 respectively. So c ∈ {0,1} (maybe also others? no, c only 0 or 1 in allowed pairs). So c must be 0 or 1.\n\nc3: variables c,d; allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,1), (0,1), (2,2), (1,0). So allowed (c,d): (0,2), (2,1), (0,1), (2,2), (1,0). So d can be 2,1,1,2,0 respectively. So d ∈ {0,1,2} but not 3? Actually d can be 0,1,2.\n\nc4: variables d,a; allowed pairs: (2,2), (3,1), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1). So allowed (d,a): (2,2), (3,1), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1). So a can be 2,1,3,0,1 respectively. So a ∈ {0,1,2,3} but only in combos.\n\nc5: variables e,f; allowed pairs: (1,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (3,0). So (e,f): (1,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (3,0). So f can be 1,2,3,2,0. So f ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nc6: variables f,g; allowed pairs: (1,0), (3,3), (0,3), (2,3), (0,1). So (f,g): (1,0), (3,3), (0,3), (2,3), (0,1). So g ∈ {0,3,3,3,1} => g ∈ {0,1,3}.\n\nc7: variables g,h; allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,0), (3,2), (3,3), (0,3). So (g,h): (0,0), (1,0), (3,2), (3,3), (0,3). So h ∈ {0,0,2,3,3} => h ∈ {0,2,3}. Also h can be 0,2,3.\n\nc8: variables h,e; allowed pairs: (0,1), (1,0), (2,0), (0,0), (1,3). So (h,e): (0,1), (1,0), (2,0), (0,0), (1,3). So e ∈ {1,0,0,0,1} => e ∈ {0,1}. So e ∈ {0,1}.\n\nc9: variables b,e; allowed pairs: (3,1), (1,0). So (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,0). So b and e are tightly coupled.\n\nc10: variables d,g; allowed pairs: (2,0), (2,3). So (d,g) must be (2,0) or (2,3). So d must be 2, and g is 0 or 3.\n\nThus, c10 forces d=2. That is a strong constraint. Let's check c10: allowed pairs: [ [2,0], [2,3] ]. So d=2, g ∈ {0,3}.\n\nThus d is fixed to 2. Good.\n\nNow c3: variables c,d: allowed pairs: (0,2), (2,1), (0,1), (2,2), (1,0). Since d=2, we need pairs where second component =2. Let's list allowed pairs with d=2:\n\n- (0,2) => c=0, d=2 works.\n- (2,1) => d=1, no.\n- (0,1) => d=1, no.\n- (2,2) => c=2, d=2 works.\n- (1,0) => d=0, no.\n\nSo allowed (c,d) with d=2 are (0,2) and (2,2). So c can be 0 or 2.\n\nThus c ∈ {0,2}.\n\nNow c2: variables b,c: allowed pairs: (3,0), (0,0), (2,0), (1,1), (2,1). So allowed (b,c) combos:\n\n- (3,0) => c=0, b=3\n- (0,0) => c=0, b=0\n- (2,0) => c=0, b=2\n- (1,1) => c=1, b=1\n- (2,1) => c=1, b=2\n\nBut we have c ∈ {0,2}. So c cannot be 1. So only pairs with c=0 are possible. So c must be 0. Because c=2 is not allowed in any pair in c2. Indeed c2 allowed c values are only 0 or 1. So c must be 0. So c=0.\n\nThus c=0.\n\nGiven c=0, from c2 we have allowed (b,c) pairs where c=0: (3,0), (0,0), (2,0). So b can be 3,0,2.\n\nThus b ∈ {0,2,3}.\n\nNow c1: variables a,b; allowed pairs: (2,3), (1,1), (0,1), (3,2), (1,0). So given b ∈ {0,2,3}, we need to find allowed a such that (a,b) is in allowed pairs.\n\nList allowed (a,b) pairs:\n\n- (2,3): a=2, b=3\n- (1,1): a=1, b=1 (b=1 not in our set)\n- (0,1): a=0, b=1 (b=1 not in set)\n- (3,2): a=3, b=2\n- (1,0): a=1, b=0\n\nThus possible combos with b ∈ {0,2,3}:\n\n- b=3 => a=2 (from (2,3))\n- b=2 => a=3 (from (3,2))\n- b=0 => a=1 (from (1,0))\n\nThus a is determined by b:\n\nIf b=3, a=2.\nIf b=2, a=3.\nIf b=0, a=1.\n\nThus a ∈ {1,2,3} correspondingly.\n\nNow c4: variables d,a: allowed pairs: (2,2), (3,1), (0,3), (3,0), (2,1). d=2. So allowed (d,a) pairs with d=2:\n\n- (2,2): a=2\n- (3,1): d=3, not relevant.\n- (0,3): d=0, not relevant.\n- (3,0): d=3, not relevant.\n- (2,1): a=1\n\nThus allowed combos with d=2: (2,2) => a=2, and (2,1) => a=1.\n\nThus a can be 1 or 2.\n\nBut from earlier, a is determined by b: if b=0 => a=1; if b=2 => a=3; if b=3 => a=2.\n\nNow we need a to be consistent with c4: a must be 1 or 2.\n\nThus the case b=2 (a=3) is invalid because a=3 not allowed by c4. So b cannot be 2. So b ∈ {0,3}.\n\nCorresponding a:\n\n- b=0 => a=1 (allowed)\n- b=3 => a=2 (allowed)\n\nThus b=0, a=1; or b=3, a=2.\n\nThus far we have:\n\nc=0, d=2, g ∈ {0,3} (from c10), c10: (d,g) must be (2,0) or (2,3). So g is either 0 or 3.\n\nNow c7: variables g,h; allowed pairs: (0,0), (1,0), (3,2), (3,3), (0,3). So given g ∈ {0,3}, we need allowed (g,h) pairs.\n\nIf g=0: allowed (0,0) and (0,3). So h can be 0 or 3.\n\nIf g=3: allowed (3,2) and (3,3). So h can be 2 or 3.\n\nThus h ∈ {0,3} if g=0; h ∈ {2,3} if g=3.\n\nNow c8: variables h,e; allowed pairs: (0,1), (1,0), (2,0), (0,0), (1,3). So allowed (h,e) combos:\n\n- (0,1): h=0, e=1\n- (1,0): h=1, e=0 (h=1 not in our possible h values)\n- (2,0): h=2, e=0\n- (0,0): h=0, e=0\n- (1,3): h=1, e=3 (h=1 not allowed)\n\nThus h can be 0,1,2. But from c7, h ∈ {0,3} or {2,3}. So possible combos:\n\n- If h=0: allowed e can be 1 (from (0,1)) or 0 (from (0,0)). So e ∈ {0,1} for h=0.\n\n- If h=2: allowed e must be 0 (from (2,0)). So e=0.\n\n- If h=3: not in c8 allowed pairs? Let's check c8: allowed pairs list does not include h=3. So h cannot be 3 because there is no allowed (3, e) pair. So h cannot be 3. So h must be 0 or 2.\n\nThus h ∈ {0,2}.\n\nThus from c7, we need to see which g values allow h=0 or h=2.\n\n- g=0: allowed h ∈ {0,3}. So h can be 0 (allowed) but not 2. So if g=0, h must be 0.\n\n- g=3: allowed h ∈ {2,3}. So h can be 2 (allowed) but not 0. So if g=3, h must be 2.\n\nThus we have two cases:\n\nCase 1: g=0, h=0.\nCase 2: g=3, h=2.\n\nNow c8 also gives e values for each h:\n\n- If h=0: e ∈ {0,1} (from (0,0) and (0,1)). So e can be 0 or 1.\n\n- If h=2: e=0 (from (2,0)). So e must be 0.\n\nThus:\n\nCase 1: (g=0, h=0) => e ∈ {0,1}.\nCase 2: (g=3, h=2) => e=0.\n\nNow c5: variables e,f; allowed pairs: (1,1), (0,2), (0,3), (1,2), (3,0). So (e,f) must be one of those.\n\nThus for each possible e, we need to find f.\n\nCase 1: e ∈ {0,1}.\n\n- If e=0: allowed (e,f) pairs: (0,2), (0,3). So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\n- If e=1: allowed (e,f) pairs: (1,1), (1,2). So f ∈ {1,2}.\n\nThus for case 1 (g=0, h=0), possible (e,f) combos:\n\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,1)\n- (1,2)\n\nCase 2: e=0 (only). So (e,f) must be (0,2) or (0,3). So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus overall possible (e,f) combos across both cases:\n\n- (0,2)\n- (0,3)\n- (1,1)\n- (1,2)\n\nNow c6: variables f,g; allowed pairs: (1,0), (3,3), (0,3), (2,3), (0,1). So (f,g) must be one of these.\n\nWe have g fixed to either 0 or 3.\n\nLet's list allowed (f,g) pairs:\n\n- (1,0): f=1, g=0\n- (3,3): f=3, g=3\n- (0,3): f=0, g=3\n- (2,3): f=2, g=3\n- (0,1): f=0, g=1 (g=1 not possible)\n\nThus for g=0, allowed f must be 1 (since only (1,0) matches). So if g=0, f must be 1.\n\nFor g=3, allowed f can be 3,0,2 (from (3,3), (0,3), (2,3)). So f ∈ {0,2,3} for g=3.\n\nNow combine with earlier possible (e,f) combos.\n\nCase 1: g=0, h=0, e ∈ {0,1}, f must be 1 (from c6). So f=1.\n\nNow check if (e,f) combos include f=1. Yes, (1,1) is allowed. Also (0,2) etc are not. So only e=1, f=1 works.\n\nThus in case 1, the only valid assignment is (e=1, f=1). Check c5: (1,1) is allowed. So case 1 yields (e=1,f=1,g=0,h=0). So tuple (e,f,g,h) = (1,1,0,0).\n\nCase 2: g=3, h=2, e=0, f ∈ {0,2,3} (from c6). But also need (e,f) pair to be allowed by c5. Since e=0, allowed (e,f) combos are (0,2) and (0,3). So f must be 2 or 3. So f ∈ {2,3}.\n\nThus possible (e,f,g,h) combos in case 2:\n\n- (e=0, f=2, g=3, h=2) => (0,2,3,2)\n- (e=0, f=3, g=3, h=2) => (0,3,3,2)\n\nNow check c6 for each:\n\n- (f=2,g=3): allowed (2,3) is in c6. Yes.\n- (f=3,g=3): allowed (3,3) is in c6. Yes.\n\nThus both are valid.\n\nNow we also need to check c9: variables b,e; allowed pairs: (3,1) or (1,0). So (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,0). So we need to ensure that b and e satisfy this.\n\nWe have two possible b values: b=0 (with a=1) or b=3 (with a=2). And e values: in case 1 e=1; case 2 e=0.\n\nThus check:\n\n- Case 1: e=1, b can be? Options: b=0 or b=3. (b,e) must be either (3,1) or (1,0). So (b=3, e=1) matches (3,1). So b must be 3. b=0 would give (0,1) not allowed. So b must be 3. So case 1 forces b=3, a=2.\n\n- Case 2: e=0, b must be? (b,e) must be (1,0) (since (3,1) not possible). So b must be 1. But b can only be 0 or 3 from earlier constraints. So b=1 is not allowed. Therefore case 2 is invalid because b cannot be 1.\n\nThus only case 1 yields a valid full solution.\n\nThus the only full solution is:\n\n- b=3, a=2 (since b=3 => a=2 from c1)\n- c=0, d=2, g=0, h=0, e=1, f=1.\n\nCheck all constraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) = (2,3) allowed? Yes (2,3) is in c1.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (3,0) allowed? Yes (3,0) is in c2.\n\nc3: (c,d) = (0,2) allowed? Yes (0,2) is in c3.\n\nc4: (d,a) = (2,2) allowed? Yes (2,2) is in c4.\n\nc5: (e,f) = (1,1) allowed? Yes (1,1) is in c5.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (1,0) allowed? Yes (1,0) is in c6.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (0,0) allowed? Yes (0,0) is in c7.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (0,1) allowed? Yes (0,1) is in c8.\n\nc9: (b,e) = (3,1) allowed? Yes (3,1) is in c9.\n\nc10: (d,g) = (2,0) allowed? Yes (2,0) is in c10.\n\nAll constraints satisfied.\n\nThus the only full solution yields right component tuple (e,f,g,h) = (1,1,0,0). So the projection onto right component has exactly one tuple: [1,1,0,0].\n\nThus the answer: payload.claim.value.tuples should contain a list with that tuple. Sorted lexicographically (only one). So we need to output JSON envelope with that tuple.\n\nThe envelope template includes fields: \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\", \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\", \"payload\": {...}, \"problem_id\": \"csp-5122\", \"read_refs\": [...], \"recipients\": [\"left-reviewer\",\"shared-integrator\"], \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"right-proposer\". The payload.claim.value.tuples currently has placeholder [[0,0,0,0]]; we need to replace with our tuple.\n\nWe must edit only payload.claim.value.tuples. So we produce the envelope with the tuple replaced.\n\nThus final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"PROPOSE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"claim\": {\n      \"claim_id\": \"right-tuples\",\n      \"dependencies\": [\n        {\n          \"claim_revision\": 1,\n          \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5122\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"provenance\": {\n        \"message_id\": \"csp-5122-right-proposer-1\"\n      },\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1,\n      \"status\": \"active\",\n      \"value\": {\n        \"component\": \"right\",\n        \"tuples\": [\n          [\n            1,\n            1,\n            0,\n            0\n          ]\n        ],\n        \"variables\": [\n          \"e\",\n          \"f\",\n          \"g\",\n          \"h\"\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5122\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5122\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    },\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"claim:right-tuples\",\n      \"revision\": 0,\n      \"state_revision\": 0\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"left-reviewer\",\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"right-proposer\"\n}\n\nWe must ensure proper JSON formatting: no trailing commas, proper quoting.\n\nThus final answer is exactly that JSON.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1438,"completion_tokens":5298,"total_tokens":6736,"cost":0,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"cache_write_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0,"video_tokens":0},"cost_details":{"upstream_inference_cost":0,"upstream_inference_prompt_cost":0,"upstream_inference_completions_cost":0},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3144,"image_tokens":0,"audio_tokens":0}}}